          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#linear_symplectomorphisms Linear symplectomorphisms
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\omega)
            regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            form, under composition, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+group"} symplectic group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(V,\omega)
          %p
            The linear Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms are also known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Hamiltonian matrices
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hamiltonian+matrices"} ?
          %h3#a_curious_example_volumes_of_balls A curious example: volumes of balls
          %p
            The following example, due to Andreas Blass and Stephen Schanuel, is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorified
            way to calculate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volumes"} volumes
            of even-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balls"} balls
          %p
            In any dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            (with respect to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  vol(B_n) = \frac{\pi^{n/2}}{\Gamma(\frac{n}{2} + 1)}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is the Euler
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gamma+function"} Gamma function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi π
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  vol(B_{2 n}) = \frac{\pi^n}{n!}
          %p
            Meanwhile, we may regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^n
            as the volume of the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polydisc"} polydisc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
            cartesian power of the complex 1-disc
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo :
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{2} = \{z: {|z|} \leq 1\}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            acts by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permuting
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2^n/S_n
            is clearly
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^n/n!
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem_blassschanuel Theorem (Blass-Schanuel)
            %p
              Given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (z_1, \ldots, z_n) \in \mathbb{C}^n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_j
              in polar coordinate form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi j
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi θ
                            %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_j = r_j e^{i \theta_j}
              = succeed "-invariant" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon B_2^n \to B_{2 n}
              by first permuting the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_j
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≥
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi …
                    %mo ≥
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_1 \geq r_2 \geq \ldots \geq r_n
              and then mapping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (z_1, \ldots, z_n)
              to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 3
                          %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %msubsup
                          %mi r
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %mi …
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %mi …
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\sqrt{r_1^2 - r_2^2}e^{i\theta_1}, \sqrt{r_2^2 - r_3^2}e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2)}, \ldots, \sqrt{r_{n-1}^2-r_n^2}e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2 + \ldots + \theta_{n-1})}, r_n e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2 + \ldots + \theta_n)})
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              induces a continuous well-defined map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2^n/S_n \to B_{2 n}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (z_1, \ldots, z_n)
              for which the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_j
              are all distinct,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              induces a smooth symplectic isomorphism mapping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n/S_n
              onto the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (w_1, \ldots, w_n) \in B_{2 n}
              for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mi j
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_j \neq 0
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi j
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq j \leq n-1
          %p
            In other words, writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_j = x_j + i y_j
            the symplectic 2-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi j
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{j=1}^n d x_j \wedge d y_j = \sum_{j=1}^n r_j d r_j \wedge d\theta_j
          %p
            is preserved by pulling back along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon P_n/S_n \to Q_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n
            are almost all of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2^n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{2 n}
            respectively, this gives a proof that the volume of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{2 n}
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi n
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^n/n!
            (alternate to standard purely computational proofs).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+transformation"} canonical transformation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equations"} Hamilton's equations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+integrator"} symplectic integrator
          %h2#references References
          %p Lecture notes include
          %ul
            %li
              Augustin Banyaga,
              %em Introduction to the geometry of hamiltonian diffeomorphisms
              (
              %a{:href => "https://www.personal.psu.edu/auw4/dakar.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The example of volumes of balls is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Andreas Blass, Stephen Schanuel, On the volumes of balls (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.lsa.umich.edu/~ablass/vol.ps"}> ps
              ).
